A DROPPED LIST LEAVES A BLANK LINE BEHIND, SO format IS NOT IDEMPOTENT.

WHAT md DOES TODAY. The list loses its only item and becomes an empty block,
but the block still contributes its separator, so the output is:

    "# Title\n\n"

Run `format` again over that and the trailing blank line goes:

    "# Title\n"

Two runs, two different answers. Anyone piping `md format` over a directory
gets a diff on the first pass and another diff on the second.

WHAT IT SHOULD DO. Keep the item, as `format-keeps-an-empty-list-item` says,
and the question of an empty list block does not arise here at all.

BOTH HALVES ARE WORTH FIXING. Even once the item is kept, a list block holding
no items should write nothing rather than a bare separator, or this returns
the moment any other path produces one.

CAUSE. Two places, and both are needed.
  - `MarkdownParser.collectListItems` drops the empty item; see the marker on
    `format-keeps-an-empty-list-item` for the guard that does it.
  - The list case in `BlockFormatter.format` appends nothing for an items
    array that is empty, but whatever joins the formatted blocks still puts a
    blank line after it, because it has a block in hand and no way to know the
    block wrote nothing.

The second is why the expected output of a fix should be checked with `wc -c`
rather than by eye: the whole defect is one byte that no diff renders.

NOT caused by `ListItem.continuation`, and not fixed by it. Both behaviours
predate that flag.
